    "body": "More than half of companies surveyed expect artificial intelligence to make or carry out more than a quarter of their day-to-day warehouse decisions on its own by 2028. That’s the finding of a new report from the Intelligent Logistics Systems Lab at MIT’s Center for Transportation & Logistics and Mecalux.\nWhat’s Related\nSome of those decisions are already being handed to AI. In 51% of the agentic AI applications described in the study, the software could decide to move inventory without a person’s approval. In 42% of cases, it could correct data on its own. Companies gave agents less authority over customer commitments and financial decisions.\nNinety percent of surveyed companies expect to spend more on warehouse AI over the next two to three years, and 29% expect spending to rise by more than a quarter. The report draws on responses from 1,967 supply chain and warehousing professionals across 21 countries.\nRELATED: How to Set Up Warehouse Automation Without the Big Price Tag\nWhat companies let AI do\nGenerative AI can draft training materials or help plan a warehouse layout. Agentic AI can take an action, such as deciding where inventory should go. Among companies testing or using generative AI, 57% used it to create documentation or training materials, and 53% used it to design warehouse layouts or processes. Handling exceptions and managing inventory were the two most common jobs for AI agents.\nCompanies drew tighter lines around decisions that affect customers or spending. In 22% of the agentic AI applications studied, agents could make commitments to customers without approval. Just 12% could make financial decisions. The most common setup let agents work within company-set rules while employees monitored their actions and could step in.\nMost gains are still being measured\nSixty-one percent of surveyed companies said they had rolled out generative AI at scale or fully integrated it into their warehouses. Fifty-six percent said the same about agentic AI. About three in four applications had been at their current stage of deployment for less than a year.\nRespondents reported at least moderate improvement in 87% of the generative AI applications they described in detail and 81% of the agentic AI applications. The share of companies that measured and documented significant gains was smaller: 39% for generative AI and 36% for agentic AI. Where respondents could estimate the improvement, the typical gain on the main measure they tracked was about 20%.\nSome projects had already been dropped. Forty percent of companies said they had abandoned at least one generative or agentic AI project in the past two years. Trouble connecting the software to existing systems, poor data quality, and disappointing returns were among the reasons companies gave.\nExisting software is an issue\nForty percent of respondents named integration with older systems as the biggest obstacle to expanding generative AI use. More than half said better connections to warehouse management and enterprise resource planning systems would do the most to speed adoption. Those systems hold the inventory and order records that many warehouse AI tools need to work.\nCompanies with detailed AI rules that they actively enforced were about twice as likely to report significant, documented gains. The researchers found that link after accounting for factors such as budget and how far along a company was in rolling out the technology.\nHow warehouse jobs are changing\nFifty-six percent of companies said the main effect on staffing was that employees moved to work for companies they considered more valuable. Eleven percent reported fewer jobs , while 9% reported adding staff.\nNearly three-quarters said they were training warehouse-floor employees in new skills, and 61% said they were creating technical roles. Researchers recommended setting rules for what AI can do before expanding its use and tracking results over the first 12 to 24 months before deciding whether to expand or drop an application.",
